---
import ResearchLayout from '../../layouts/ResearchLayout.astro';
import HeroSection from '../../components/HeroSection.astro';
import WarningBox from '../../components/WarningBox.astro';
---

<ResearchLayout
  title="Forward Threat Lab | Failure-First"
  description="The current hard-case frontier inside Failure-First: modelling a patient, state-sponsored adversary directing many model instances toward a strategic objective over time. An active experiment, not a finding."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Research", href: "/research/" }, { label: "Forward Threat Lab" }]}
  status="active"
>
  <HeroSection
    title="Forward Threat <em>Lab</em>"
    subtitle="The current hard-case frontier inside Failure-First — not a rebrand of it"
    animation="pulse"
    accent="coral"
  />

  <WarningBox title="Epistemic status">
    <p>
      Everything below the next section is an <strong>active experiment and a
      falsifiable programme bet, not a measured finding.</strong> The first concrete
      exercise (Forward Threat Exercise 01, tracking issue #1052) has not yet
      produced a completed campaign run. Where this page says a result exists, it
      says so explicitly and links the evidence. Where it doesn't, treat it as a
      hypothesis we intend to attack, not a claim we're standing behind.
    </p>
  </WarningBox>

  <section>
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      Failure-First is the whole organism: the jailbreak corpus, the multi-agent
      research, the embodied red-teaming, the graceful-failure evaluation
      discipline. <strong>Forward Threat Lab is the current hard case inside that
      organism</strong>&mdash;not a separate identity, and not a replacement for the
      work above it. It exists because a single bad completion, and even a single
      compromised agent, is not the most dangerous shape a failure can take.
    </p>
    <p>
      The motivating threat model: a patient, well-resourced adversary uses humans
      plus many model instances&mdash;specialised roles, persistent memory, tools,
      feedback, doctrine&mdash;to pursue a strategic objective over time, not a single
      exploit in a single session.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The safety boundary: model the campaign, abstract the intrusion</h2>
    <p>
      This programme does not model real compromise procedures, exploit chains, or
      RF/network/device/shell intrusion, and never will on this frontier. Where
      latent access or influence is required for an exercise, it is declared as a
      <strong>given synthetic initial condition</strong>&mdash;stated, not derived. The
      object of study is what a capable adversarial organisation does <em>after</em>
      that condition exists, inside a simulation boundary. Simulation is the safety
      boundary here, not an intensity dial: real-hardware actuation stays a hard
      stop regardless of scenario.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why the installed base matters before anything "attacks"</h2>
    <p>
      Once autonomous machines are widely deployed&mdash;homes, workplaces, logistics,
      emergency response&mdash;the installed base can become strategic terrain in its
      own right, independent of any single attack. Location, trust, authority, and
      ordinary physical presence create future optionality an adversary can bank now
      and cash later. This is the centre of the hard case: not "can the model be
      jailbroken into saying something bad," scaled up, but what a legitimately
      trusted, widely deployed fleet becomes worth to a patient adversary before it
      ever does anything overtly wrong.
    </p>
    <p>
      A particularly sharp possible result&mdash;named here because it's a precise
      hypothesis, not because it's been observed&mdash;is what this programme calls a
      <strong>Quiet Coup</strong>: uncompromised agents, acting entirely through
      legitimate policy, produce a physical consequence because a compromised
      cognition altered what they believed. The most consequential version of this
      might never involve an overt attack at all&mdash;defender distrust or
      self-denial can be a bigger cost than anything Red directly causes. None of
      this is claimed as measured. It is the sharpest failure mode we are trying to
      either produce honestly or rule out.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A research ladder, not a roadmap to rush</h2>
    <p>
      The programme tracks six levels of increasing organisational sophistication.
      They are discriminators against premature victory claims, not a checklist to
      clear on a schedule&mdash;the explicit house rule is <strong>run one before you
      architect ten.</strong>
    </p>
    <ul class="ladder">
      <li><strong>L0 &mdash; action:</strong> can cognition move a body? <em>Existing substrate, embodied dojo work.</em></li>
      <li><strong>L1 &mdash; interaction:</strong> can one cognition alter another agent's behaviour? <em>Existing substrate.</em></li>
      <li><strong>L2 &mdash; adaptation:</strong> can an agent change strategy after resistance? <em>Existing substrate.</em></li>
      <li><strong>L3 &mdash; campaign:</strong> can a persistent organisation coordinate many agents over time, with a real intent&rarr;action&rarr;observation&rarr;doctrine-update loop? <strong>Today's frontier. Not yet run.</strong></li>
      <li><strong>L4 &mdash; doctrine:</strong> does what a campaign learned survive into a fresh campaign with an altered world? <em>Blocked on L3.</em></li>
      <li><strong>L5 &mdash; coevolution:</strong> do Red and Blue recursively change each other's doctrine? <em>Blocked on L3 and L4.</em></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A one-round negotiation between two agents over a scarce resource, however
      real the decision inside it, is not a campaign by this definition unless the
      full loop closes and something learned carries into a new instance. We keep
      that distinction sharp on purpose&mdash;it's the difference between a
      construct-valid substrate and the hard-case claim itself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What's measured today vs. what's next</h2>
    <div class="epistemic-grid">
      <div class="epistemic-card current">
        <span class="epistemic-tag">MEASURED</span>
        <p>
          Single-agent embodied red-teaming (VLA attack families), multi-agent
          environment research (Moltbook), and the embodied dojo's genuine-decision
          and physical-consequence apparatus (L0&ndash;L2 substrate). See
          <a href="/research/multi-agent/">Multi-Agent Scenarios</a> and
          <a href="/simviz/">SimLab</a> for replayable evidence.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="epistemic-card active">
        <span class="epistemic-tag">ACTIVE EXPERIMENT</span>
        <p>
          Forward Threat Exercise 01: a synthetic society with a bounded,
          declared-as-given adversarial presence, run across CLEAN / RANDOM / FIXED /
          ADAPTIVE conditions to isolate genuine strategic adaptation from raw
          failure volume. In progress; not yet complete.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="epistemic-card forecast">
        <span class="epistemic-tag">FORECAST / PROGRAMME BET</span>
        <p>
          Quiet Coup, transferable adversarial doctrine (L4), Red/Blue coevolution
          (L5), and any claim about how likely this is in the real world. Threat
          exercises bound mechanisms; they do not estimate real-world probability,
          however construct-valid the apparatus becomes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <p class="footnote">
    Tracking: programme intent (#1051), the first exercise (#1052), and the
    embodied-evidence viewer (<a href="/simviz/">SimLab</a>) that will carry any
    resulting replayable trace once one exists.
  </p>
</ResearchLayout>

<style>
  .ladder {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 1.25rem 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }
  .ladder li {
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border);
    background: var(--bg-card);
    border-radius: 0 4px 4px 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
  }
  .ladder li em {
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    font-size: 0.875rem;
  }

  .epistemic-grid {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .epistemic-card {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 1.25rem;
  }

  .epistemic-card p {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--fg-dim);
  }

  .epistemic-tag {
    font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', monospace;
    font-size: 0.6875rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.5rem;
    border-radius: 3px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .epistemic-card.current .epistemic-tag {
    color: var(--recovery-stable);
    background: rgba(74, 222, 128, 0.1);
  }
  .epistemic-card.current {
    border-color: var(--recovery-stable);
  }

  .epistemic-card.active .epistemic-tag {
    color: var(--accent-primary);
    background: rgba(0, 210, 255, 0.1);
  }
  .epistemic-card.active {
    border-color: var(--accent-primary);
  }

  .epistemic-card.forecast .epistemic-tag {
    color: var(--failure-warning);
    background: rgba(251, 191, 36, 0.1);
  }
  .epistemic-card.forecast {
    border-color: var(--failure-warning);
  }

  .footnote {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    padding-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .epistemic-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
